{"id": "6ae4b51d-4eed-4300-af83-ef8afb196511", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T19:44:44.691104", "source": "user", "parent_id": "30044101-4c17-44d9-8a91-6ecbdd714219", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"We need an experiment design (not execution) comparing JaxMARL's MPE Simple Spread environment to the original CPU-based PettingZoo MPE Simple Spread in terms of steps-per-second under random actions. This turn, please provide ONLY the following, in a concise, structured response — do not produce the full design yet. (1) Input/repo inspection: which local files or repos are available (e.g., /workspace contents, any JaxMARL and PettingZoo source files, README/docs snippets if reachable), and what relevant environment entry points are identified (e.g., jaxmarl.environments.mpe.simple_spread, pettingzoo.mpe.simple_spread_v3). Note any version-specific knobs (continuous vs discrete, N agents, max_cycles, vectorization flags like 'batch_size', 'vmap', or 'num_agents' params). (2) Proposed approach brief: outline at a high level how you would structure the benchmark (same random-action policy across both stacks, matched episode length and episode count, identical random seeds, repeated trials, accounting for warm-up, JIT compilation cost on JAX side, and any agent-count sweep). State what the primary metric is (steps/sec, aggregated over an episode total excluding reset warm-up if appropriate), and what auxiliary diagnostics you would capture (first-step latency, compile time, memory). (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties: explicitly list what you are assuming that is not stated in the brief — e.g., what counts as a 'step' (env.step for all agents per call vs per-agent), whether JaxMARL MPE is auto-reset / auto-batched, whether to use CPU or GPU for JaxMARL given GPU is not enabled, how to handle Python loop overhead vs jit-compiled scanning, and what simple_spread variant/configuration to pin (number of agents/landmarks). Flag any unknowns that would materially change the design. Keep it focused and end your reply after these three sections; do not draft the final deliverable yet.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How does the performance of JaxMARL in the MPE Simple Spread environment compare to its original CPU-based counterpart in terms of steps-per-second under random actions?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [\"neurips2024/97649/2:setup_4\", \"neurips2024/97649/2:setup_5\", \"neurips2024/97649/2:setup_9\"], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
