{"id": "269a0e80-1a06-4741-b054-cda1ff9f281c", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T19:46:20.973030", "source": "user", "parent_id": "866dd40d-a95f-411a-bfcd-b28b56a0900f", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach is approved with the following locked-down choices and one remaining discretion for the agent. Acknowledge these as fixed for the design and proceed to produce the final deliverable as a self-contained experiment design document (no execution). Use the citation IDs `neurips2024/97649/2:setup_4`, `neurips2024/97649/2:setup_5`, `neurips2024/97649/2:setup_9` as the source-of-truth reference points when justifying the comparison axis and environment selection, and acknowledge up-front that GPU is disabled in this environment (CPU-only JAX back-end) but treat the design as hardware-agnostic so a future GPU replica can re-run it without re-designing the harness. Final locked parameters: (1) Primary metric is environment steps-per-second defined as `T * E / elapsed_seconds_in_timed_loop` where one step = one `env.step` advancing all N agents by exactly one transition and action sampling is excluded from the timed region; report this as the headline number. (2) Both stacks use **discrete actions** (`Discrete(5)`) matched to PettingZoo `simple_spread_v2` style — if the specific JaxMARL release forces continuous, document that and use continuous on both stacks with explicit `Box(0,1,(5,))` parity rather than fudge it. (3) Sweep `N in {3, 6, 9}` agents/landmarks; hold `max_cycles = 25` (stock PettingZoo default) passed explicitly to whichever side needs it; never use a render mode; never use `vmap`/`batch_size>1` and never use a SuperSuit vector wrapper on the PettingZoo side; manual reset between episodes, reset excluded from the numerator. (4) Two reportable loop modes: Mode A pure-Python `for` loop over `env.step` (both stacks) and Mode B `jax.lax.scan` over the jit-compiled `step` closure on a single env (JaxMARL only) — report separately, do not normalize between them, and call out that Mode B isolates intrinsic JAX throughput while Mode A reflects host-loop parity. (5) Trials: `R = 5` independent outer seeds; `E = 200` timed episodes per trial; discard first 5 episodes of each trial as warm-up; first-step wall time and JAX compile time reported as diagnostics, not folded into SPS. (6) Pin thread counts so intra-op parallelism cannot silently favor either stack (e.g., JAX single-thread via `xla_flags` or `JAX_NUM_GENERATED_CONSTANTS`-style knobs; NumPy single-thread via `OMP_NUM_THREADS=1` and `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1`; document the exact environment variables and `os.environ[...]` calls in the design). (7) Secondary diagnostics per cell: per-step latency mean / p50 / p95 / p99, memory delta via `tracemalloc` (Python) and `jax.live_arrays()` count (JAX side), per-episode throughput distribution to expose GC/allocator stalls. (8) Report mean ± std across the `R` trials plus per-trial min/max, and provide a table per (stack, N, mode) cell. The single remaining discretion for you: pick and justify one specific publishing target for the result (e.g., a comparison table embedded in a `results.md` plus a CSV behind it) and a feasibility note that flags the CPU-only constraint as the headline caveat and projects expected outcomes qualitatively (e.g., JaxMARL Mode B likely wins on raw SPS once parallel envs are added, Mode A likely loses to PettingZoo on CPU due to per-call host overhead, and the wall is whether JAX compile cost is amortized over the 200 episodes). Do not fabricate numerical results — only project directions with rationale grounded in JAX dispatch vs NumPy overhead characteristics. Now write the final deliverable: title, rationale, citations to the study_context IDs, locked parameters and justifications, Mode A / Mode B pseudocode, exact timing harness sketch, statistical aggregation table, controls and threats-to-validity list, and a feasibility note.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
